      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To identify risk indicators for, and incidence of, recurrent falls and fall-related injuries in wheelchair users with traumatic spinal cord injury."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective multi-centre study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "One hundred and forty-nine wheelchair users with spinal cord injury attending follow-up in Sweden and Norway."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Inclusion criteria: wheelchair users ≥\u200918 years old with traumatic spinal cord injury ≥\u20091 year post-injury."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "EXCLUSION CRITERIA",
          "content": "individuals with motor complete injuries above C5. Falls were prospectively reported by text message every second week for one year and were followed-up by telephone interviews. Outcomes were: fall incidence, risk indicators for recurrent (>\u20092) falls and fall-related injuries. Independent variables were: demographic data, quality of life, risk willingness, functional independence, and exercise habits."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Of the total sample (n\u2009=\u2009149), 96 (64%) participants fell, 45 (32%) fell recurrently, 50 (34%) were injured, and 7 (5%) severely injured. Multivariate logistic regression analysis showed that reporting recurrent falls the previous year increased the odds ratio (OR) of recurrent falls (OR 10.2, p\u2009<\u20090.001). Higher quality of life reduced the OR of fall-related injuries (OR 0.86, p\u2009=\u20090.037)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Previous recurrent falls was a strong predictor of future falls. The incidence of falls, recurrent falls and fall-related injuries was high. Hence, prevention of falls and fall-related injuries is important."
        }
      ]},
